in @prism

# @mirror/glass — the substrate-altitude primitive.
#
# A glass is the abstract type-pattern of a parseable surface in
# mirror. The keyword `grammar` has been the bootstrap surface for
# this notion since v0; the substrate-altitude truth surfaced in the
# 2026-05-26 conversation (Alex + Reed) is that everything declared
# as `grammar @X { ... }` was already a glass — a transparent,
# refractive surface through which source bytes settle into AST.
#
# See docs/insights/2026-05-26-fixed-and-the-spectral-feedback-
# fracture.md for the substrate-altitude decisions. The rename
# `grammar => glass` runs via @kintsugi/fracture/grammar-to-glass
# in a follow-up tick (Stage 2 of #94). For Stage 1, the substrate
# stays bootstrap-parseable: `grammar` is the surface keyword; the
# meaning is glass.
#
# This file declares the glass-type pattern itself — the abstract
# shape every parseable surface in mirror inhabits. Concrete glasses
# (@mirror/glass/ast, @mirror/match, @mirror/nl, @code/rust, ...)
# instantiate the pattern; the substrate is what gives them all the
# same algebra.
#
# The two file-level predicates `is_mirror(g)` and `namespace(g)`
# that live in the legacy @mirror/grammar are NOT redeclared here —
# they are operations on a glass, not part of the glass-type itself,
# and land at @mirror/glass/ast.

grammar @mirror/glass {
  # A glass: the abstract type of a parseable surface. Today this is
  # equivalent to the `grammar` keyword's denotation; the alias names
  # the substrate-altitude truth so downstream substrates can speak
  # in the language the corpus has been writing in all along.
  type glass
}

out glass
out @mirror/glass
